    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349601",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f4b340f6-0ac6-5bbf-a7d4-0088ca3c4e5e",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=4x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-{e}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-e\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "e^9-e"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "e^9-e",
          "choices_complete": false,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
