    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349646",
      "knowledge_point_id": "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=x-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{5}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}({5}^{1}-{5}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
