      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {6}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {6}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\frac{{6}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
