      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "$\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=5x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{5x}}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
