    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-30973",
      "knowledge_point_id": "19fd901f-e08d-54c5-9cda-68543a3d71a9",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=6x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of  $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(729-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{728}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
